# Shared local development defaults. Each service also has its own .env.example.
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=debug

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Option A — local Postgres via docker compose (default for quick local dev):
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql://armman:armman@localhost:5432/arogya?schema=public
#   DIRECT_URL=postgresql://armman:armman@localhost:5432/arogya?schema=public
#
# Option B — Supabase (hosted Postgres). With 16 services you MUST use the
# transaction pooler for runtime and the direct connection for migrations:
#   DATABASE_URL -> Supabase "Transaction pooler" URL (port 6543) + pgbouncer flags
#   DIRECT_URL   -> Supabase "Direct connection" URL (port 5432), used by db push
# Both are under: Supabase Dashboard > Project Settings > Database > Connection string.
#
#   DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1
#   DIRECT_URL=postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://armman:armman@localhost:5432/arogya?schema=public
DIRECT_URL=postgresql://armman:armman@localhost:5432/arogya?schema=public

# Redis (docker-compose). Supabase has no Redis — keep local, or use managed Redis
# (e.g. Upstash) in cloud environments.
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JWT auth (RS256, per the HLD §4.2)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JWT_PRIVATE_KEY / JWT_PUBLIC_KEY are NOT set here — they are per-service:
#   apps/auth-service/.env.example — private + public key, signs tokens
#   apps/api-gateway/.env.example  — public key only, verifies tokens
# Generate a local dev keypair with:
#   openssl genpkey -algorithm RSA -pkeyopt rsa_keygen_bits:2048 -out private.pem
#   openssl pkey -in private.pem -pubout -out public.pem
# Use the SAME keypair for both services — the gateway must verify tokens
# auth-service actually signs.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Demo Quick Response seed data (optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Set these to also seed one sample REOPEN, ACCOMPANIED_REFERRAL, and
# REFERRAL_INCOMPLETE Quick Response card (apps/closure-reopen-service/prisma/seed.ts,
# apps/risk-referral-service/prisma/seed.ts). Unset (default) — those 3 seed steps
# just log "skipped" and every other seed step is unaffected.
#
# Unlike every other seed step, these make real HTTP calls through the gateway, so
# api-gateway/auth-service/approval-service must be running and reachable at
# API_GATEWAY_BASE_URL. `npm run seed` does not auto-load this file — export these in
# your shell (or `set -a; source .env; set +a`) before running it.
#
# SEED_DEMO_BENEFICIARY_ID — an existing beneficiary_cases.beneficiary_id.
# SEED_DEMO_SAKHI_USER_ID  — an existing users.user_id for a SAKHI (requestedByUserId
#                            on all 3 cards).
# SEED_DEMO_AUTH_TOKEN     — a bearer JWT (no "Bearer " prefix) for a SAKHI or
#                            SUPERVISOR user, e.g. from POST /api/v1/auth/login.
# SEED_DEMO_BENEFICIARY_ID=
# SEED_DEMO_SAKHI_USER_ID=
# SEED_DEMO_AUTH_TOKEN=
